abletim — programmatic manipulation of Ableton Live sets through scripting.
(Developed interactively via realisateur /ideate, 2026-07-25 — vision below
is decided-vs-open-tagged, not just a raw drop.)

Vision: script-level access to Ableton Live projects, serving my own
production workflow and batch/catalog processing across many sets for now.
Scope is expected to grow into all of it eventually (generative/algorithmic
composition, session-clip live workflows) — those are explicitly NOT
decided yet, don't build toward them.

Entry paths (decided): two, in parallel for the first milestone —
(a) offline .als parsing: .als is gzipped XML; parse/rewrite with Live
    closed. Undocumented schema, shifts between Live versions — this half
    is reverse-engineering-flavored.
(b) Max for Live / Live Object Model: runtime access from inside a device
    (js/v8 or patching), reads live_set locators, can create/manipulate
    clips while Live runs.
External OSC/Python bridges (AbletonOSC/pylive style): considered, not
chosen for now.

Flagship use case: slice audio at arrangement location markers (locators).
Chosen outputs: (1) each marker-to-marker region cut to its own audio file
on disk (sample-library/batch half), (2) a new derived .als whose clips
are the slices (set-as-source-material half). Session clips in the
original set: offered, not chosen for v1.

Milestone chain (hybrid probe-then-commit):
1. (current) One bounded spike per entry path against the slicing use
   case. Offline spike: gunzip a real .als, find locators + audio clip
   references in the XML, map locator beat-positions to audio-file sample
   offsets, cut regions with ffmpeg/sox. M4L spike: a minimal device that
   reads live_set locators and proves clip creation. Each spike ends in a
   short findings note, not a polished tool.
2. Commit to the winning path; working marker-slicer end-to-end, audio
   files on disk first.
3. Derived-.als generation from the slices.
4. (not queued) generative direction, session-clip workflows,
   catalog-wide batch operations over many sets.

Host / deployment (decided 2026-07-25, same session): abletim is a
dexter-pinned project. Ableton itself (Windows app) and the media library
live on dexter — this meets `_paced.dexter.conf`'s stated pinning bar
(hardware/network-evidenced, like crt; unlike wtul's named exception)
cleanly. Bootstrap shape, following the crt precedent exactly:
- Scaffold on mandark as usual (repo at ~/Documents/Projects/abletim,
  bare remote at ~/git-remotes/abletim.git), but REPO_URL in
  schedule/abletim.conf should be
  ssh://mandark-lan/home/zach/git-remotes/abletim.git so dexter's
  scheduler can clone it (the git-shell-only dexter_mandark_deploy path,
  live-verified for crt 2026-07-24).
- Rotation line goes in _paced.dexter.conf ONLY (never also _paced.conf —
  no double dispatch), with the hardware evidence stated in the comment.
- Cross-host mechanics (decided 2026-07-25, Zach-directed — full entry in
  realisateur .claude/FOCUS.md same date): mandark→dexter batch SSH is
  live-verified (lands in Windows PowerShell; `wsl -d Ubuntu -u zach`
  reaches /home/zach/scheduler and /mnt/c/Users/Zach) and Zach licensed
  it NARROWLY — a dedicated restricted key + forced-command gateway
  (reverse analog of dexter_mandark_deploy), not the existing full zach
  shell. Until that restricted channel is provisioned AND live-verified
  (does what it should, refuses what it shouldn't), the dexter rotation
  line + sync happens in a human dexter-side session, crt/wtul style.
  Either way the edit executes ON dexter, keeping the "dexter writes
  _paced.dexter.conf" concurrency rule intact.
- Hold the crt bar, learned again the hard way with wtul: enabled=1 only
  after a live `git ls-remote` of the abletim remote FROM dexter itself
  succeeds — don't land the line on the strength of mandark-side setup.
- What unattended dexter runs can do: the offline .als spike (WSL2
  reaches the Windows media via /mnt/c/...; treat real sets/media as
  READ-ONLY, work on copies/fixtures — same spirit as wtul's
  don't-touch-the-real-drive rule). What they can't: the M4L/LOM spike
  needs Ableton open with a human present — that half is
  interactive-session work, not batchable.

Blockers / open questions for milestone 1:
- Provision + live-verify the narrow mandark→dexter channel (dedicated
  keypair on mandark, forced command= in dexter's Windows-side
  authorized_keys wrapping a small-verb gateway) — human-present
  interactive session, never unattended; key material + another host's
  authorized_keys is exactly what nightly runs must not touch. Fallback
  until then: human dexter-side session for the registration half.
- Confirm the Windows-side path to the Ableton projects/media on dexter
  (the /mnt/c/... equivalent) — human-only, needed before the offline
  spike can point at anything real.
- Which Live version(s) to target — schema and LOM both vary. Human-only:
  Zach to confirm his Live version and whether he has Max for Live
  (Suite or M4L license) before the M4L spike is buildable.
- Warp/tempo mapping risk (offline path): locators live in beat time,
  audio files in sample time; warp markers mediate. If this mapping turns
  out hairy, it's the likeliest reason the offline spike loses.
- Needs a sample .als (with locators + audio) as a test fixture — human
  drop, can't be synthesized credibly.
